  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Federal contract obligations in Nevada total $3.4B for FY2026 to date. Obligations are down 15.6% year over year and the state has recorded 21,233 awards with an average award size of $160K. The Department of Defense is the largest buyer with $1.3B in obligations, followed by the Department of Energy with $1.1B and the General Services Administration with $560M. Facilities Support Services is the largest industry by obligations at $1.1B, with Engineering Services second at $625M and Automobile Manufacturing third at $456M. Mission Support & Test Services LLC is the top vendor with $997M, followed by Fleet Vehicle Source Inc with $456M and JT4 LLC with $385M. Historical obligations peaked at $4B in FY24 and FY25, then moved to $3.4B in FY26. All figures are contract obligations and current-year totals are partial (fiscal year to date).",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies are driving federal spending in Nevada?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense obligated $1.3B in FY2026 to date. The Department of Energy obligated $1.1B and the General Services Administration obligated $560M. The Department of the Interior, the Department of Veterans Affairs, and domestic agencies round out the top buyers with obligations between $134M and $20M."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Who and what capture the biggest shares of Nevada contract dollars?",
        "body": "MISSION SUPPORT & TEST SERVICES LLC received $997M, the largest vendor share. Facilities Support Services received $1.1B in industry obligations, while Engineering Services received $625M and Automobile Manufacturing received $456M. The top vendors and top industries together explain a large portion of the $3.4B in total obligations."
      }
    ]
  },
